|> Exercise
    !! work=ing
    handle=_more_closely_related_to_sin2x_or_

    Based on the graphs of Exercise >>_four_graphs_,
    or some other logic,
    does it seem more likely that 
    $x \ra \cos^2 x$, $x \ra \sin^2 x$ and $x \ra \cos x\sin x$ 
    are more closely related to
    $$
    \sin(x/2)
    $$
    and/or $\cos(x/2)$,
    or to
    $$
    \sin(2x)
    $$
    and/or $\cos(2x)$?

    |> Solution

        One can see from the graphs of $\cos^2$, $\sin^2$
        and $\sin\cdot\cos$ that these functions cycle “twice as
        fast” as sine or cosine, just like $x \ra \sin(2x)$
        and $x\ra \cos(2x)$ cycle twice as fast. The answer is
        therefore: the latter. (I.e., “$\sin(2x)$ and/or $\cos(2x)$”.)
        !! “$\sin(2x)$ and/or $\cos(2x)$”.
        !! $\sin$ or $\cos$.

        |> SolutionNote

            More mathematically, say that $f : \rr \ra \rr$

            __has period $T$__

            or

            __is periodic mod $T$__

            for some number $T \in \rr$ if

            $$
            f(x + T) = f(x)
            $$

            for all $x \in \dom f$. Then $\sin$, $\cos$ are

            __periodic mod $4\eta$__

            while $\sin^2$, $\cos^2$ and $\sin\cos$ are not
            only periodic mod $4\eta$ but accomplish the further
            feat of being

            __periodic mod $2\eta$__

            since, for example,

            $$
            \sin(x + 2\eta)\cos(x + 2\eta) = (-\sin(x))(-\cos(x)) = \sin x\cos x
            $$
            |> ImageLeft
                src=images/e5_more_closely_related_to_sin2x_or_left_2eta_cloud.svg
            !! |> ImageRight
            !!     src=images/e5_more_closely_related_to_sin2x_or_right_2eta_cloud.svg

            for all $x \in \rr$, which establishes the periodicity of $\sin\cdot\cos$ mod
            $2\eta$, etc.

